{'_client': <twikit.client.client.Client object at 0x000001E534E2AE40>, '_data': {'__typename': 'Tweet', 'rest_id': '1878872668340199932', 'core': {'user_results': {'result': {'__typename': 'User', 'id': 'VXNlcjoxNTIyMzgxMDQ0ODk2MzMzODI1', 'rest_id': '1522381044896333825', 'affiliates_highlighted_label': {}, 'has_graduated_access': True, 'is_blue_verified': False, 'profile_image_shape': 'Circle', 'legacy': {'can_dm': False, 'can_media_tag': False, 'created_at': 'Fri May 06 01:02:26 +0000 2022', 'default_profile': True, 'default_profile_image': False, 'description': 'Proud member of the EGA- Semper Fi.  Love my country,  disappointed in our government. Enjoying the show from a mountain in Oregon... underground man', 'entities': {'description': {'urls': []}}, 'fast_followers_count': 0, 'favourites_count': 210117, 'followers_count': 5002, 'friends_count': 7232, 'has_custom_timelines': False, 'is_translator': False, 'listed_count': 20, 'location': 'by the river, under a tree. ', 'media_count': 5469, 'name': 'Feeling Free', 'normal_followers_count': 5002, 'pinned_tweet_ids_str': ['1619139617374240768'], 'possibly_sensitive': False, 'profile_banner_url': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_banners/1522381044896333825/1730934930', 'profile_image_url_https': 'https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1854303196057137152/nkaN-Pn9_normal.jpg', 'profile_interstitial_type': '', 'screen_name': '1x2_many', 'statuses_count': 78584, 'translator_type': 'none', 'verified': False, 'want_retweets': False, 'withheld_in_countries': []}, 'professional': {'rest_id': '1685373305329754112', 'professional_type': 'Creator', 'category': [{'id': 477, 'name': 'Professional Services', 'icon_name': 'IconBriefcaseStroke'}]}}}}, 'unmention_data': {}, 'edit_control': {'edit_tweet_ids': ['1878872668340199932'], 'editable_until_msecs': '1736796726000', 'is_edit_eligible': False, 'edits_remaining': '5'}, 'is_translatable': False, 'views': {'count': '616', 'state': 'EnabledWithCount'}, 'source': '<a href="http://twitter.com/download/android" rel="nofollow">Twitter for Android</a>', 'note_tweet': {'is_expandable': True, 'note_tweet_results': {'result': {'id': 'Tm90ZVR3ZWV0OjE4Nzg4NzI2NjgwNTQ5OTQ5NDU=', 'text': "The Ticking Time Bomb of California's FAIR Plan: Navigating the Wildfire Insurance Crisis\n\nNot going to discuss how they fires started, the lack of preparedness and resources, the defunding of mitigation, who is at fault... I am going to share the concern of the impact all of that above has on your property and homes in CALIFORNIA- we need leadership\n\nIn the sun-bathed landscapes of Southern California, where wildfires now rage with alarming regularity, a financial storm brews beneath the surface. The California FAIR Plan, the state's insurer of last resort, stands at the epicenter of this storm, grappling with liabilities that could shake the foundations of the entire insurance industry in the Golden State.\n\nA Growing Liability\n\nThe FAIR Plan, designed to offer basic fire coverage to homeowners shunned by private insurers, has seen its exposure soar to startling heights. As of late 2024, the plan's total liability was pegged at over $458 billion, with a particularly concentrated risk in areas like Pacific Palisades, where its exposure alone is estimated at $5.89 billion. This isn't just a number; it's a reflection of a crisis where private insurers are retreating from high-risk zones, leaving the FAIR Plan to bear the brunt. The number of policies under the FAIR Plan has surged, now exceeding 450,000, marking a 123% increase in just three years.\n\nThe Reserves and Reinsurance Dilemma\n\nWith only around $700 million in cash reserves, the FAIR Plan's financial armor seems thin against the backdrop of potential wildfire devastation. To bolster its defenses, the plan has secured about $2.5 billion in reinsurance, with a total limit of $4.85 billion under certain conditions. However, these numbers tell a story of vulnerability; the plan's per-occurrence retention stands at $900 million, a figure that could be obliterated by a single catastrophic fire season.\n\nWhat If the Plan Can't Cover Its Liability?\n\nHere's where the plot thickens. If a wildfire of unprecedented scale hits, and the FAIR Plan's reserves and reinsurance are depleted, the fallout would be significant:\n\nAssessment on Insurers: The FAIR Plan's structure allows it to pass the financial hat around, assessing contributions from every property and casualty insurer in California. This could mean substantial financial hits for these companies, potentially affecting their solvency.\n\nSpike in Premiums: The financial strain would likely translate into higher premiums or surcharges for all policyholders in California, not just those in high-risk areas, making insurance less affordable across the board.\n\nMarket Exodus: The risk of insolvency for some insurers, particularly smaller ones, could lead to a further contraction of the insurance market in California, pushing more homeowners into the arms of the already stretched FAIR Plan or leaving them uninsured.\n\nState Intervention: The crisis might necessitate state intervention, perhaps through new legislation or direct financial support, though this would be politically and financially fraught.\n\nInsurance Market Stability: The overarching concern is the stability of the insurance market itself. With the FAIR Plan's potential failure to cover its liabilities, the very notion of insurability in California could come into question, deterring new insurers and exacerbating the crisis.", 'entity_set': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': []}, 'richtext': {'richtext_tags': []}}}}, 'legacy': {'bookmark_count': 1, 'bookmarked': False, 'created_at': 'Mon Jan 13 18:32:06 +0000 2025', 'conversation_id_str': '1878862392026964238', 'display_text_range': [14, 290], 'entities': {'hashtags': [], 'symbols': [], 'timestamps': [], 'urls': [], 'user_mentions': [{'id_str': '69156861', 'name': 'Chief Nerd',